An optimal and clean approach to solve this problem is to iterate through the array, maintaining the frequencies of the numbers and the counts of those frequencies.

For each prefix of length $L$ (where $L$ goes from $1$ to $n$):
- Keep track of `count[x]`, the frequency of number $x$.
- Keep track of `freq[f]`, the number of distinct elements that have frequency $f$.
- Keep track of `max_f`, the maximum frequency of any element in the current prefix.

A prefix of length $L$ is valid if removing exactly one element leaves all remaining elements with equal frequencies. This occurs under any of the following mutually exhaustive conditions:
1. `max_f == 1`: Every distinct element appears exactly once. Removing any one element leaves the remaining distinct elements each with a frequency of 1.
2. `freq[max_f] * max_f + 1 == L` and `freq[1] == 1`: There is exactly one element with frequency 1, and all other elements have frequency `max_f`. Removing that single element leaves all remaining elements with frequency `max_f`.
3. `freq[max_f] == 1` and `freq[max_f - 1] * (max_f - 1) + max_f == L`: There is exactly one element with frequency `max_f`, and all other elements have frequency `max_f - 1`. Removing one occurrence of this element leaves all remaining elements with frequency `max_f - 1` (this also correctly handles the case where all elements in the prefix are identical).

### C++ Source Code

