An optimal and elegant solution in C++.

### Method Explanation

The problem asks for the minimum area of an axis-aligned rectangle formed by 4 points from the input. 
Given $N \le 500$, we can consider every pair of points $(x_1, y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2)$ as potential diagonal corners:
1. Two points can form a diagonal of an axis-aligned rectangle with non-zero area if and only if $x_1 \ne x_2$ and $y_1 \ne y_2$.
2. The remaining two corners of this rectangle must be $(x_1, y_2)$ and $(x_2, y_1)$.
3. If both of these points exist in the set, we calculate the area $|x_1 - x_2| \times |y_1 - y_2|$ and update the minimum area found so far.

To quickly check if a point exists:
We can encode each point $(x, y)$ as a 64-bit integer `((long long)x << 32) | y` and store them in a sorted vector. With $N \le 500$, `std::binary_search` takes only about $\approx 9$ comparisons and has excellent cache locality, avoiding the overhead of hash sets. We can also prune pairs whose area is already greater than or equal to the minimum area found.

### C++ Source Code

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int minAreaRect(vector<vector<int>>& points) {
        int n = points.size();
        vector<long long> encoded_points(n);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            encoded_points[i] = ((long long)points[i][0] << 32) | (unsigned int)points[i][1];
        }
        sort(encoded_points.begin(), encoded_points.end());

        int min_area = INT_MAX;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            int x1 = points[i][0], y1 = points[i][1];
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; ++j) {
                int x2 = points[j][0], y2 = points[j][1];

                if (x1 == x2 || y1 == y2) continue;

                int area = abs(x1 - x2) * abs(y1 - y2);
                if (area >= min_area) continue;

                long long p3 = ((long long)x1 << 32) | (unsigned int)y2;
                long long p4 = ((long long)x2 << 32) | (unsigned int)y1;

                if (binary_search(encoded_points.begin(), encoded_points.end(), p3) &&
                    binary_search(encoded_points.begin(), encoded_points.end(), p4)) {
                    min_area = area;
                }
            }
        }

        return min_area == INT_MAX ? 0 : min_area;
    }
};
```

